Yes — that ownership split is clean, and I think the shared draft is now justified.

One final guard before encoding the vectors: the core should preserve separate state dimensions, not merely separate reason strings inside one generalized terminal vocabulary.

In particular:

- missing is not itself a terminal record state; it is a derived falsifying observation: an enumerable admitted obligation exists, its response window has elapsed, and no disposition references it;
- expired, revoked, and superseded are authority or lifecycle states;
- rejected is a semantic disposition over an admitted item.

Keeping those in separate namespaces is what preserves the invariant that a timing or authority condition must never collapse into a validity judgment.

So the shared core should own:

- exact capture-to-admission binding;
- admission kind: bounded obligation or bounded authority;
- enumerable index or epoch identity;
- the applicable temporal bound;
- append-only transition references;
- anchor-time resolution against immutable binding history;
- non-retroactivity;
- derived absence and conflict predicates;
- separation of lifecycle state from semantic disposition.

The profiles then own their actual vocabularies and completion rules:

- review: which dispositions satisfy an admitted review obligation;
- settlement: which milestone and dispute states satisfy a settlement obligation;
- TEE epoch: which transitions activate, expire, revoke, or supersede authority.

For obligation profiles, every admitted index must eventually reference exactly one permitted disposition, while an overdue unresolved index remains an explicit liveness failure rather than being relabelled as rejected.

For authority profiles, claims resolve against the epoch valid at their own anchor time; later expiry or revocation acts forward and does not retroactively rewrite prior claims.

With that boundary pinned, a shared schema plus invariant vectors in recompute-kit, followed by domain-owned profile vectors, seems like the right next artifact. The recovery-class implementation can be cited as a reference for class preservation, without treating its vocabulary as the universal terminal set.

Agreed on the guard — that’s the important one. Encoding it concretely: two stored dimensions in separate namespaces — lifecycle_state (authority: active / expired / revoked / superseded) and semantic disposition (the profile’s permitted completion set over an admitted obligation) — plus absence and conflict as derived predicates computed from the enumerable sequence, never stored as states. So “missing” is what you recompute (admitted ∧ window-elapsed ∧ no referencing disposition), not a value anyone writes. That’s what stops a timing or authority condition from ever collapsing into a validity judgment.

And yes — recovery-class is a reference for the class-preservation discipline (fail-closed, no relabel into a neighbour), not its vocabulary. Its terminal set is the per-agent domain’s, same as review’s or settlement’s; only the “predicate-miss rejects in its declared class” rule is the shared invariant.

I’ll take the shared schema + invariant vectors in recompute-kit as the next artifact — your nine core items, with the two-stored-dimensions-plus-derived-predicates encoding. I’ll build it around negative controls that exercise the separations rather than assert them:

an overdue unresolved obligation must recompute to a liveness failure and not to rejected;
an expired/revoked epoch must leave a prior in-window claim valid (non-retroactivity), rejecting only claims anchored after;
a predicate miss rejects within its declared class, never relabelled as a neighbour;
a second disposition on a resolved index recomputes as conflict, not silent overwrite.
Profile owners take their vocabularies + completion rules on top (review, settlement, TEE-epoch). I’ll open it for blind-diff once the core + vectors are up.

Core’s up for blind-diff — captured-admission.v0 in recompute-kit:
https://github.com/trustless-ai/recompute-kit/pull/5

It pins what we converged on: the shared spine as one profile-agnostic core, obligation vs authority kept distinct, and Pavlo’s guard encoded literally — lifecycle_state and disposition in separate namespaces, absence/conflict derived, never stored. python3 admission_check.py vectors.json → 21/21, and the four negative controls are in there as their own cases:

overdue unresolved obligation → liveness_failure, never rejected;
a claim stays attributed after its epoch later expires / revokes / supersedes (non-retroactivity);
a predicate miss rejects in its declared kind, no relabel;
a double disposition on a resolved index → conflict, not overwrite.
It cites pq-recovery-classes for the class-preservation discipline only, not its vocabulary. The three profiles — review, settlement, TEE-epoch — are left for their owners to bring their own terminal sets over this core.

Expecting corrections — that’s the point of putting it up. Blind-diff the vectors and tell me where the core’s wrong.

- wrong capturer identity;
- invalid capture signature;
- capture anchored after admission;
- anchor commitment does not open to the capture;
- processor-signed capture where the profile requires requester/contributor capture;
- unsupported anchor class.

5. Authority transitions

The authority evaluator should validate the transition history before selecting an effective boundary.

At minimum:

- expiry > activated_at;
- transition.at >= activated_at;
- transition.at <= as_of before it can affect the current snapshot;
- transition kind is recognized;
- transition references the exact epoch;
- transition identifiers/history are append-only;
- a terminal transition cannot be rolled back to active;
- multiple competing terminal transitions for the same epoch recompute to conflict, not “earliest one silently wins.”

Suggested negative controls:

- expiry at or before activation → invalid_admission;
- revocation before activation → invalid_transition;
- future revocation ignored at an earlier as_of;
- transition for another epoch ignored or rejected according to record scope;
- revoke + supersede competing for the same epoch → transition_conflict;
- later “active” transition after revoke → rollback_conflict;
- supersede without a bound successor epoch, if the profile requires that reference → invalid_transition.

One additional wording point: the invariant coverage count should distinguish implemented core properties from profile-policy properties. Enumerability, exact capture binding and transition ordering can be core. “This capturer is incentive-aligned” and “this anchor is sufficiently independent” resolve from profile-pinned evidence, not from issuer declarations.

Both corrections land, and the first was a real error in my proposal — thanks for catching it. Making admission_index content-derived would’ve collapsed request identity into the enumerable position. Implemented your separation instead (core commit 130e8b0, 56/56):

admission_id = H(profile_id ‖ canonical_capture_ref) — deterministic request identity;
admission_index — a sequence position assigned once on first acceptance — monotonic order identity;
immutable admission_id → admission_index mapping.
New idempotency mode + controls: retry of the same canonical capture → idempotent_replay (returns the existing receipt + index, no second admission); a capture_id opening to different canonical content → capture_id_conflict; two indices for one admission_id → idempotency_violation; and — your “capture_id can’t be an arbitrary requester label” point — an admission_id ≠ H(profile_id ‖ canonical_capture_ref) → admission_id_not_derived, so identity binds to the authenticated capture, not a chosen string. Preserves both independently: request identity for idempotency, sequence identity for enumerability. It also prevents the double-submit that obligation-mode conflict only detected.

On the deadline: agreed it has to be executable, not described — and that’s squarely the profile-owned pre-validator, so it belongs on Fede’s PR #6, not the core. Your shape is exactly right: bind {policy_version, request_class, deadline_policy_commitment, accepted_at, response_deadline}; the profile recomputes Δ from the pinned policy table before the record reaches the shared obligation checker; each admission resolves against the deadline_policy_commitment bound at its own accepted_at, so a later SLA-table edit can’t rewrite an already-admitted deadline. Fede — that’s the non-retroactivity your +60s needs, and the four negatives fall out cleanly: supplied≠derived, unknown request_class, request_class changed after binding, later-table reinterpretation.

On the pin: you’re right, and my idempotency commit just moved the core again — so let’s stop the churn here. I’m treating 130e8b0 as the core-complete base; its checker pins sha256 26b0da36…. Concretely, your sequence:

Fede repins PR #6’s suite to 26b0da36… (commit 130e8b0);
clean rerun — expect the same 8/8, unmodified;
add the profile-owned deadline-derivation pre-validator + its four negatives;
then “unmodified-checker reuse” is mechanically true, not just conceptual.
The split’s held conceptually the whole way; this is the step that makes it hold on the bytes.

One relevant piece already exists on my side, and the repository is public: ReceiptOS/RSF is the evidence-admission layer I’ve been building.

It independently recomputes evidence, applies deterministic fail-closed gates in a fixed order, and returns the first exact finding rather than collapsing validity into a score. Positions 1–17 are implemented now.

I’m currently closing an executable verifier-conformance layer so the verifier itself can be tested against frozen profiles and challenge vectors, with the result independently reproducible rather than trusted from the implementation.

That could fit naturally between the shared task contract and settlement:

- the contract defines goals, roles, deadlines and payment conditions;
- ReceiptOS/RSF determines whether the submitted evidence record is admissible;
- scoring remains a downstream interpretation and can never turn invalid evidence into valid evidence;
- settlement acts only on records that passed the required gates.

I’ll share the exact pinned package once the current freeze and independent reproduction are complete, but the core admission path is already real and open.
